{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfmth7nGwZenKmqbEKdc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: Dq ≈ 427 cm⁻¹ (Δt ≈ 4 270 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 899 cm⁻¹. C cannot be determined from these bands.**\n\n**Approach.** Tetrahedral d⁸ is the hole analogue of octahedral d², so it has the same energy-level pattern. The ground state is ³T₁(F), and the three spin-allowed bands are assigned as:\n\n- ν₁ = ³T₁(F) → ³T₂ = 3 600 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ³T₁(F) → ³A₂ = 7 870 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ³T₁(F) → ³T₁(P) = 16 410 cm⁻¹\n\nThe two ³T₁ states mix. Diagonalising that 2×2 block gives these expressions, where R = √(225B² + 180·Dq·B + 100Dq²):\n\n- ν₁ = 5Dq − 7.5B + R/2\n- ν₂ = 15Dq − 7.5B + R/2\n- ν₃ = R\n\n**Step 1: find Dq.** Subtracting the first two gives ν₂ − ν₁ = 10Dq. So Dq = (7 870 − 3 600)/10 = 427 cm⁻¹, and Δt = 10Dq ≈ 4 270 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Step 2: find B.** Setting R = ν₃ = 16 410 and putting it into the ν₁ expression gives 7.5B ≈ 6 740, so B ≈ 899 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Step 3: check with a least-squares fit.** Fitting all three bands at once gave Dq = 427.3 cm⁻¹ and B = 899.1 cm⁻¹. The fitted positions are 3 598, 7 871 and 16 410 cm⁻¹, with an RMS error of about 1 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Checks:**\n- The Dq/B ratio is about 0.48, which is reasonable for a tetrahedral complex.\n- The free-ion Ni²⁺ value is B ≈ 1 080 cm⁻¹, so the ratio β ≈ 0.83 points to moderate covalency.\n\n**Why C is left out.** The three spin-allowed bands are not affected by the Racah parameter C. The fit had to assume C ≈ 3 800 cm⁻¹, so that number is not a result.\n\n{\"Dq\": 427, \"B\": 899, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Three spin-allowed bands of Td d8 (d2-like) fix Dq and B; C is not determined by spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1847,"cache_read_input_tokens":7975,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1847,"ephemeral_1